= Theory of mind — Technical =

== Representational problem ==
Mental-state reasoning requires tracking representations that may differ from reality:

<pre>
world state W
agent A believes B
B may differ from W
</pre>

A system must prevent its own knowledge from automatically replacing the attributed state.

== Explicit and implicit tasks ==
Elicited-response false-belief tasks require language, memory, inhibition, and response selection.

Non-elicited paradigms reduce some demands but introduce interpretive questions about looking time, expectation, learned behavior, and low-level cues.

== Competing architectures ==
Accounts include theory-theory, simulation theory, modular systems, two-systems accounts, predictive models, and broader social-learning approaches.

No one architecture defines the construct.

== Measurement purity ==
Theory-of-mind tasks often mix mental-state representation with attention, inference, retrieval, pragmatics, and motor response.

Comparing populations or artificial systems therefore requires matching non-ToM task demands where possible.

